## 18. Acceptance criteria

The completed application must satisfy the following requirements:

1. The interface renders correctly and looks polished on desktop and mobile.
2. Every navigation item opens the correct page.
3. Users can create, edit, save, schedule, reschedule, and delete content.
4. Daily posting schedules are configurable independently for TikTok and Facebook.
5. The backend can persist scheduled jobs and process them without the browser remaining open.
6. Platform publishing is implemented through supported official APIs when configured.
7. Incoming comments and supported direct messages can be synchronized from connected accounts.
8. Reply templates and keyword rules can be configured and tested.
9. Human-review actions work and persist.
10. Analytics charts respond to filters and display the correct dataset.
11. CSV export produces a valid downloadable file.
12. Google Sheets export works when correctly configured.
13. Activity logs record workflow outcomes and failures.
14. Demo mode is clearly distinguished from real operations.
15. Missing permissions and credentials produce actionable error messages.
16. No interactive controls are decorative or inert.
17. The project includes documented setup instructions and a sample environment file.
18. No feature falsely claims that a real post, reply, connection, or export succeeded.

## 19. Implementation strategy

Build the actual application, not just a description of it.

Follow this sequence:

Phase 1: Establish the React application, shared layout, navigation, design system, and responsive behavior.

Phase 2: Implement the dashboard, content studio, publishing calendar, and persistent content state.

Phase 3: Implement the unified inbox, configurable response templates, keyword rules, and human-review workflows.

Phase 4: Implement analytics charts, CSV export, and the Google Sheets integration structure.

Phase 5: Implement the official API integration layer, account connection states, server-side publishing jobs, and background workers.

Phase 6: Implement settings, activity logs, onboarding, readiness checks, and documentation.

Phase 7: Validate every workflow, fix runtime errors, verify responsive behavior, and refine visual details.

If the environment supports multi-file editing, organize the code into sensible modules and components. Do not place the entire application in one giant file.

Do not stop after generating the initial dashboard. Continue implementing the remaining pages and workflows until the acceptance criteria are met.

If the output or generation environment imposes limits, prioritize a working, coherent end-to-end core and then continue with the remaining modules. Do not silently omit features.

